  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1050 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nMonday night through Thursday:\n\nECMWF and GFS Ensemble output appears much lest organized for\nthe trough and low pressure system we were expecting midweek \nnext week, yielding some serious concerns for predictability,\nbut the general consensus remains the same for this forecast\nperiod: The ridge over the plains finally breaks down, and a\ntrough or open wave (of unknown amplitude) in the jet kick\nstarts a more active / occasionally rainy weather pattern for \nmidweek and beyond across the Midwest. \n\nFor Monday night into Tuesday, models have fairly good agreement\non a positively-tilted trough digging east from Nevada into New\nMexico, inducing broad low-pressure over the Great Plains,\nworking with high pressure over the Ohio Valley to drive\nincreasing southerly winds. WAA driven rain chances build west\nto east into the CWA through the day Tuesday, with rain looking\nlikely (60-70% chances) across the region Tuesday night. \n\nWednesday and onwards is where predictability falls off sharply.\nThe 18z GFS allows the aforementioned trough to produce a\nhealthy surface low and track it straight towards the region,\nyielding shower and perhaps even thunderstorm chances mid to\nlate week. The ECMWF model partially closes off the New Mexico\ntrough into an upper low and stalls it there, preventing or\nsubstantially delaying moisture return for the midwest (and\nyielding lulls in the activity mid to late week). Until things\nbecome clearer, our forecast will reflect broad-brushed 30-50%\nrain chances across southern WI mid to late week. \n\nSheppard",
